We start with the 23 global secrets (automatic replication). The earlier test showed what to do: copy each into a regional `me-central2` secret, switch the app to read from there, then remove the global copies later. Steps 1 and 2 only add regional copies, so the running app keeps reading the global secrets and nothing changes for it yet.

**Where to run it:** on the backend server, since that's where the regional copy worked before. If gcloud says `Reauthentication required`, run `gcloud auth login --no-launch-browser` first. These commands never print a secret value.

## Step 1: inventory (read-only)

```bash
P=pg-ae-n-app-125609; L=me-central2
rg() { CLOUDSDK_API_ENDPOINT_OVERRIDES_SECRETMANAGER="https://secretmanager.$L.rep.googleapis.com/" gcloud "$@"; }
echo "== GLOBAL SECRETS =="; gcloud secrets list --project=$P --format="table(name.basename(),createTime.date(),replication)"
echo "== REGIONAL SECRETS =="; rg secrets list --location=$L --project=$P --format="table(name.basename(),createTime.date())"
echo "== GROUP SIZES =="; gcloud compute instance-groups managed list --project=$P --format="table(name,size,targetSize,instanceTemplate.basename())"
```
This shows exactly which secrets exist, and that only `CN_STAGING_MAIL_DRIVER` is regional so far. It also shows the group sizes, which decide when you can push a deploy.

## Step 2: copy every `CN_STAGING_*` secret to `me-central2`

Run this after step 1. It's safe to re-run. It skips secrets that already exist, and prints only names and a result:
```bash
P=pg-ae-n-app-125609; L=me-central2
rg() { CLOUDSDK_API_ENDPOINT_OVERRIDES_SECRETMANAGER="https://secretmanager.$L.rep.googleapis.com/" gcloud "$@"; }
for s in $(gcloud secrets list --project=$P --filter="name~CN_STAGING_" --format="value(name.basename())"); do
  if ! rg secrets describe $s --location=$L --project=$P >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    rg secrets create $s --location=$L --project=$P >/dev/null || { echo "CREATE FAILED $s"; continue; }
    gcloud secrets versions access latest --secret=$s --project=$P | rg secrets versions add $s --location=$L --project=$P --data-file=- >/dev/null || { echo "ADD FAILED $s"; continue; }
  fi
  a=$(gcloud secrets versions access latest --secret=$s --project=$P | sha256sum)
  b=$(rg secrets versions access latest --secret=$s --location=$L --project=$P | sha256sum)
  [ "$a" = "$b" ] && echo "OK $s" || echo "MISMATCH $s"
done
echo "global: $(gcloud secrets list --project=$P --filter='name~CN_STAGING_' --format='value(name)' | wc -l)  regional: $(rg secrets list --location=$L --project=$P --format='value(name)' | wc -l)"
```
Expect `OK` for all 23 and equal counts at the end. That also settles the 5 secrets the app doesn't read: copying them leaves nothing global to remove later.

## Step 3: switch the app, one commit in the backend repo

You'll need both edits together. The compiled loader builds its endpoint as `${location}-secretmanager.googleapis.com`, and that returned 404 in our test, so the app can't use regional secrets until the endpoint form changes.

**1. `src/common/config/secret-manager.ts`.** Find the line with `apiEndpoint` and change only that string:
```ts
// before
apiEndpoint: `${location}-secretmanager.googleapis.com`
// after
apiEndpoint: `secretmanager.${location}.rep.googleapis.com`
```
I haven't seen the TypeScript source, only the compiled JavaScript, so match its exact formatting. With `SECRET_LOCATION` unset the code path is unchanged.

**2. `ecosystem.config.js`.** Add one line to `env_staging`:
```js
      SECRET_LOCATION: 'me-central2',
```

**Condition for pushing:** step 2 must show all `OK`, and the backend group must be at size 1, because a rollout with `--max-surge=3` stacks on extra servers. If your apt fix isn't pushed yet, pushing the backend repo now will include it, which is fine, but check the group size first.

**The Packer build is the safety test.** The build VM starts the app with `SECRET_LOCATION=me-central2`. The app throws if any of its 18 secrets can't be read, so `/health` fails and the build stops before any server is replaced. A passing build with `APP IS UP` proves the regional path works for the service account too. I haven't proven that account's access yet. The project-level secretAccessor role should cover regional secrets, but that's an assumption.

## Step 4: remove the global copies, later

Only after the new server has run for days. I'll give you a reversible trial first (disable the global versions, confirm a fresh boot still works), then the delete.

## Effects and rollback

| | |
|---|---|
| Downtime | none |
| New cost | regional secrets are new items, small, and I haven't verified the rate |
| Steps 1 to 2 rollback | delete the regional secrets (Console → Secret Manager → Regional secrets) |
| Step 3 rollback | revert the commit and push. The global secrets still exist, so the old behavior works |
| Step 4 | ⚠️ deleting a secret can't be undone |

## One operational change to tell the team

After the switch, anyone who rotates a secret must add the new version to the **regional** secret. A new version on the global copy won't be seen by the app. Document that, and tell me if someone manages these secrets automatically elsewhere.

